1. Consider the circle divided by points \( A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}, \ldots, A_{n} \) into equal parts, and let \( P \) be an arbitrary point on the circumference of the circle.

2. To show that the sum of the squares of the distances from \( P \) to each point \( A_i \) is constant, we start by analyzing the areas of triangles \( PA_1A_2 \), \( PA_2A_3 \), and so on. Denote the area of triangle \( PA_{1}A_{2} \) by \( t_{1} \), \( PA_{2}A_{3} \) by \( t_{2} \), ..., \( PA_{n-1}A_{n} \) by \( t_{n-1} \), and \( PA_{n}A_{1} \) by \( t_{n} \).

3. By the given conditions, the angle at point \( P \) inside each triangle is \( \alpha \). Thus, the areas of these triangles can be expressed as:
   \begin{align*}
   t_{1} &= \frac{1}{2} \overline{PA_{1}} \cdot \overline{PA_{2}} \sin \alpha, \\
   t_{2} &= \frac{1}{2} \overline{PA_{2}} \cdot \overline{PA_{3}} \sin \alpha, \\
   & \vdots \\
   t_{n-1} &= \frac{1}{2} \overline{PA_{n-1}} \cdot \overline{PA_{n}} \sin \alpha, \\
   t_{n} &= \frac{1}{2} \overline{PA_{n}} \cdot \overline{PA_{1}} \sin \alpha. \\
   \end{align*}

4. Using Carnot's Theorem, specifically stating that for any triangle \( PA_i A_{i+1} \):
   \begin{align*}
   \overline{PA_i}^2 + \overline{PA_{i+1}}^2 = \overline{A_i A_{i+1}}^2 + 2 \overline{PA_i} \cdot \overline{PA_{i+1}} \cos \alpha.
   \end{align*}

5. Summing these equalities for all triangles \( PA_{i}A_{i+1} \):
   \begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=1}^{n} \overline{PA_i}^2 &= \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( \overline{A_i A_{i+1}}^2 + 2 \overline{PA_i} \cdot \overline{PA_{i+1}} \cos \alpha \right).
   \end{align*}

6. Since the sum of the products \( \overline{PA_i} \cdot \overline{PA_{i+1}} \sin \alpha \) can be related to the sum of the areas of these triangles:
   \begin{align*}
   2 \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \overline{PA_i}^2 \right) &= n \overline{A_1 A_2}^2 + 2 \cos \alpha \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \overline{PA_i} \cdot \overline{PA_{i+1}} \right).
   \end{align*}

7. Using trigonometric identities and the fact that the total sum of areas is equal to \( T_n \), the area of the regular polygon, we have:
   \begin{align*}
   2 \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} \overline{PA_i}^2 \right) &= n \overline{A_1 A_2}^2 + 4 \operatorname{ctg} \alpha \cdot T_n.
   \end{align*}

8. Substituting \(A_1 A_2 = 2r \sin \alpha \) and the area of the regular polygon:
   \begin{align*}
   T_n &= n \frac{\overline{A_1A_2}^2}{4 \operatorname{tg} \alpha} = n r^2 \sin^2 \alpha \operatorname{ctg} \alpha, \\
   4 \operatorname{ctg} \alpha \cdot T_n &= 4 n r^2 \cos^2 \alpha.
   \end{align*}

9. Hence, the sum of the squares becomes:
   \begin{align*}
   \sum_{i=1}^{n} \overline{PA_i}^2 &= \frac{1}{2} \left[4 n r^2 \left( \sin^2 \alpha + \cos^2 \alpha \right) \right] = 2 n r^2.
   \end{align*}

10. This proves that the sum of the squares of the distances from \(P\) to each point \(A_i\) is constant, as stated:
   \begin{align*}
   \boxed{2 n r^2}.
   \end{align*}

Conclusion. \( \boxed{ { \overline{P A_1} }^2 + { \overline{P A_2}  }^2 + \ldots + { \overline{P A_n}  }^2 = 2 n r^2 } \)

